course: world
lesson: 23
title: Flowers, pollination and how seeds travel
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A flower is how a plant makes seeds. In its middle are the stamens, which make a fine powder called pollen, and the pistil, which holds tiny eggs called ovules.
explain: Pollination happens when pollen gets carried from the stamens to the sticky top of a pistil, usually of another flower of the same kind; bees, butterflies, birds and the wind carry it.
explain: After pollination, the pollen and the ovule join (fertilisation), the ovule becomes a seed, and the part around it grows into a fruit.
explain: Seeds must travel away from their parent, so the young plants do not compete with it for light and water: dandelion seeds fly on little parachutes, burdock burrs hook onto fur, coconuts float, and berries are eaten by birds and dropped far away.
fact: Stamens are the parts of a flower that make pollen.
fact: The pistil is the part of a flower that holds the ovules, which become seeds.
fact: Pollination is the carrying of pollen to the pistil of a flower.
fact: Bright petals, scent and nectar, a sweet sugary juice, attract insects that carry pollen.
fact: Bees feed on nectar and pollen, and carry pollen from flower to flower on their hairy bodies.
fact: Grasses and many trees are pollinated by the wind and have small, dull flowers with lots of light pollen; they make huge amounts because most of it blows away and never reaches another flower.
fact: After fertilisation the ovules become seeds and the flower's ovary, the swollen bottom part of the pistil, grows into a fruit.
fact: Seeds travel away from the parent plant so that young plants have room, light and water.
fact: Some seeds are carried by the wind, like dandelion and maple seeds.
fact: Some seeds hook onto animals' fur, like burdock burrs.
fact: Some seeds pass through animals that eat the fruit and are dropped far away.
fact: Some seeds float on water, like coconuts, and some are shot out when the pod bursts open, like peas and some beans.
exercise: What is pollination? | Carrying pollen to the pistil of a flower | Watering the flower with rain from the clouds | Seeds falling from a flower | Leaves making food in sunlight
exercise: Which part of a flower makes pollen? | The stamens | The bright petals | The pistil in the middle | The green stem
exercise: Which part of a flower becomes the seeds? | The ovules inside the pistil | The pollen on the stamens | The bright coloured petals | The green leaves below it
exercise: Why do many flowers have bright petals and a sweet smell? | To attract insects that carry pollen | To scare away hungry animals | To catch more sunlight to make food for the plant | To keep the rain off the pollen
exercise: Why do bees visit flowers? | To feed on nectar and pollen | To lay their eggs in the petals | To rest from the hot sun | To drink rainwater from leaves
exercise: How does pollen get carried by a bee? | It sticks to the bee's hairy body | The bee carries it in its mouth only | The bee rolls it into small balls of mud | It rides on the bee's wings
exercise: Which plants are mostly pollinated by the wind? | Grasses and many trees | Roses and sunflowers | Orchids and tulips | Daisies and poppies
exercise: Why do wind-pollinated flowers make huge amounts of pollen? | Most of it never reaches another flower | It is needed to colour the petals | The plant eats the extra pollen | Insects carry most of it away to eat in their nests
exercise: What grows into a fruit after pollination? | Part of the flower around the seeds | The leaves under the flower | The tip of the root | The pollen grain itself, which swells up
exercise: Why do seeds need to travel away from the parent plant? | So young plants get enough light and water | So the parent plant can eat them later when it is hungry | So they stay out of the rain | So birds cannot see where they are
exercise: How do dandelion seeds travel? | They fly on the wind with tiny parachutes | They hook onto the fur of animals | They float down rivers to the sea | They are shot out far when their dry pods burst
exercise: How do burdock burrs travel? | They hook onto animals' fur | They are blown far by the wind | They float on lakes and ponds | They roll down hills by themselves
exercise: How do coconuts spread to new islands? | They float on the sea | They are carried by birds | They fly on the wind | They are shot out of pods
exercise: How do the seeds of a cherry tree often travel? | Birds eat the fruit and drop the seeds | The wind blows the heavy stones away | The seeds float on puddles of rain | The fruit bursts and throws them far
test: A farmer's apple trees give almost no fruit one year when there are very few bees. Why? | The flowers were not pollinated | The bees usually eat the apples | Bees water the apple trees | Bees make the leaves grow
test: A seed has a light, fluffy tuft. How is it most likely spread? | By the wind | By floating on the sea | By hooking onto fur | By bursting out of a pod
test: A plant has small green flowers with no smell and no nectar. How is it probably pollinated? | By the wind | By bees | By butterflies | By hummingbirds
test: A seed has tiny hooks all over it. How does it travel? | It catches on passing animals | It is blown by the wind | It floats on water | It is eaten by fish
test: Which comes first in the life of a flower? | Pollination | The fruit ripening | The seed falling | The seed sprouting
test: Why do some plants grow sweet, colourful fruits? | Animals eat them and spread the seeds | The fruits make food from light | The fruits protect the roots | The fruits hold water for the leaves in dry weather
test: A big seed has a hard, waterproof shell and air inside, and the plant grows on beaches. How does it travel? | It floats on the sea | It flies on the wind | It hooks onto birds | It is shot from a pod
test: Pea pods dry out and suddenly twist open. What does this do? | It throws the seeds away from the plant | It pollinates the next flower | It makes the seeds inside grow much faster | It attracts bees to the plant
test: What happens inside a flower after pollen reaches the pistil? | The ovules can become seeds | The petals turn into leaves | The stamens turn into roots | The pollen becomes nectar
test: why are flowers all bright n smelly | To bring insects that carry the pollen | To scare away animals that eat them | To keep the flower warm at night | To make the seeds grow faster
test: how do dandelion seeds get about | The wind carries them | They stick to animals' fur | They float down rivers | They are shot out of a pod
test: my sister says bees just take nectar and don't help the flowers. true? | No, they carry pollen from flower to flower | Yes, bees only take food from flowers | Yes, bees harm the flowers they visit | No, bees help by eating the pests
test: Tom says grass doesn't have flowers cos it isn't green and pretty. right? | No, grass has small, dull flowers pollinated by the wind | Yes, grass never flowers at all | Yes, only bright plants have flowers | No, grass flowers are bright, but they're hidden down in the soil
exam: Identify the parts of the flower that produce pollen. | The stamens | The coloured petals | The central pistil | The green sepals
exam: Which of the following is designated by the term pollination? | The conveyance of pollen to the pistil of a flower | The dispersal of seeds by the wind | The growth of a fruit from the ovary | The germination of a seed in the soil
exam: Identify the principal function of bright petals, scent and nectar. | The attraction of insects that convey pollen | The protection of the flower from frost | The manufacture of food for the plant | The repulsion of grazing animals
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the dispersal of burdock seeds. | They are conveyed by attachment to the fur of animals | They are conveyed by the wind, being light and furnished with plumes | They are conveyed by floating upon water | They are expelled by a bursting pod
